on September 11th I'll be thinking less  about the World Trade Center and more  about my father and the relentless  probably unique ability of New York City  to bury its dead and move on without a  backward glance my father was born in  Manhattan in 1923 and a tenement  building off Columbus Circle a few years  later he moved to Brooklyn a borough  that was considered the country back  then a place that had more horses than  cars by the time he left there for good  in 1966 it wasn't the country anymore he  worked for seal and a shipping company  that was one of the world trade centers  original tenants one of my very earliest  memories is of my older brother and me  playing in the company's unfinished  offices and one of the towers before the  complex open to the public in 1973 like  many probably most New Yorkers my father  hated the towers at first preferring the  Chrysler and Empire State buildings  which had gone up during his childhood  he'd seen King Kong when it came out in  1933 he explained and he just couldn't  see the big ape climbing the towers by  the late 70s after Philippe Petit  tightrope walked across them George will  excaliferb them and King Kong himself  had been shot off them he'd come around  on a trip to Manhattan around then he  asked me if I wanted to see where he'd  been born he hadn't been back to the old  neighborhood since before the war it was  feeling nostalgic  we walked toward the circle only to  realize that not only the building he'd  been born and was gone but the entire  Street paved over sometime in the 50s or  60s in the rush to built Lincoln Center  a place he'd never think of entering as  the realization sunk in he shrugged  turned to me and said well do you want  to go see a movie instead there's  nothing that will lessen the horror of  9/11 or do justice to the murdered souls  interred forever at Ground Zero but in a  strange and beautiful and terrible way  New York and America will honor the most  by pausing only briefly to pay our  respects
 

